### What is an LFU cache?

An LFU (Least Frequently Used) cache evicts the entry with the lowest access frequency when the cache is full. Unlike LRU, which evicts the least recently used, LFU keeps entries that are accessed most often, which works well when access patterns are stable.

### How do you achieve O(1) get and put?

The trick is three data structures working together:

- A `HashMap<K, Node>` for O(1) key lookup.
- A `HashMap<Integer, DoublyLinkedList>` mapping each frequency to the list of all nodes at that frequency.
- An integer `minFreq` tracking the smallest frequency present.

The doubly linked list per frequency allows O(1) removal from the middle (each node holds pointers to its neighbors) and O(1) addition to the head. Within the same frequency, nodes are ordered by recency.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class LFUCache<K, V> {
    private final int capacity;
    private int minFreq;
    private final Map<K, Node<K, V>> keyMap;
    private final Map<Integer, DoublyLinkedList<K, V>> freqMap;

    public LFUCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.minFreq = 0;
        this.keyMap = new HashMap<>();
        this.freqMap = new HashMap<>();
    }

    static class Node<K, V> {
        K key;
        V value;
        int freq;
        Node<K, V> prev, next;

        Node(K key, V value) {
            this.key = key;
            this.value = value;
            this.freq = 1;
        }
    }

    static class DoublyLinkedList<K, V> {
        Node<K, V> head, tail;
        int size;

        DoublyLinkedList() {
            head = new Node<>(null, null);
            tail = new Node<>(null, null);
            head.next = tail;
            tail.prev = head;
            size = 0;
        }

        void addFirst(Node<K, V> node) {
            node.next = head.next;
            node.prev = head;
            head.next.prev = node;
            head.next = node;
            size++;
        }

        void remove(Node<K, V> node) {
            node.prev.next = node.next;
            node.next.prev = node.prev;
            size--;
        }

        Node<K, V> removeLast() {
            if (size == 0) return null;
            Node<K, V> last = tail.prev;
            remove(last);
            return last;
        }
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        Node<K, V> node = keyMap.get(key);
        if (node == null) return null;
        promote(node);
        return node.value;
    }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        if (capacity <= 0) return;

        Node<K, V> node = keyMap.get(key);
        if (node != null) {
            node.value = value;
            promote(node);
            return;
        }

        if (keyMap.size() >= capacity) {
            evict();
        }

        Node<K, V> newNode = new Node<>(key, value);
        keyMap.put(key, newNode);
        freqMap.computeIfAbsent(1, f -> new DoublyLinkedList<>())
               .addFirst(newNode);
        minFreq = 1;
    }

    private void promote(Node<K, V> node) {
        int oldFreq = node.freq;
        DoublyLinkedList<K, V> oldList = freqMap.get(oldFreq);
        oldList.remove(node);

        if (oldList.size == 0 && oldFreq == minFreq) {
            minFreq++;
        }

        node.freq++;
        freqMap.computeIfAbsent(node.freq, f -> new DoublyLinkedList<>())
               .addFirst(node);
    }

    private void evict() {
        DoublyLinkedList<K, V> minList = freqMap.get(minFreq);
        Node<K, V> victim = minList.removeLast();
        if (victim != null) {
            keyMap.remove(victim.key);
        }
    }
}
```

### How does get work step by step?

On `get(key)`, if the key is missing you return null (cache miss). If present, you read the value and promote the node: remove it from its current frequency list, increment its frequency, and add it to the head of the next frequency list. If the old list is now empty and was at `minFreq`, increment `minFreq`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.cache.LFUCache;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class CacheDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LFUCache<String, BigDecimal> cache = new LFUCache<>(3);

        cache.put("RAJESH-001", new BigDecimal("50000.00"));
        cache.put("PRIYA-002", new BigDecimal("75000.00"));
        cache.put("PANKAJ-003", new BigDecimal("100000.00"));

        // Access Rajesh twice: frequency becomes 3
        cache.get("RAJESH-001");
        cache.get("RAJESH-001");

        // Access Priya once: frequency becomes 2
        cache.get("PRIYA-002");

        // Put a new key: Pankaj (freq 1) is evicted (least frequent)
        cache.put("NEHA-004", new BigDecimal("25000.00"));

        System.out.println("Pankaj: " + cache.get("PANKAJ-003")); // null
        System.out.println("Rajesh: " + cache.get("RAJESH-001")); // 50000.00
    }
}
```

```text
Pankaj: null
Rajesh: 50000.00
```

### How does put work step by step?

On `put(key, value)`, if the key exists, update the value and promote. If the key is new and the cache is at capacity, evict: go to the list at `minFreq`, remove its tail (least recent among least frequent), and delete it from the key map. Then insert the new node with frequency 1 and set `minFreq` to 1.

### What is frequency decay and why does it matter?

Pure LFU suffers from cache pollution. A key that was extremely hot long ago keeps a high frequency count and never leaves, even though it is no longer accessed. Frequency decay periodically ages old counts.

A common approach is to halve every node's frequency periodically, or use a time-aware counter where recent accesses weigh more. Caffeine uses a frequency sketch with periodic aging.

```java
public void decayAll() {
    // Halve all frequencies periodically
    for (Node<K, V> node : keyMap.values()) {
        int oldFreq = node.freq;
        DoublyLinkedList<K, V> oldList = freqMap.get(oldFreq);
        if (oldList != null) oldList.remove(node);

        node.freq = Math.max(1, node.freq / 2);
        freqMap.computeIfAbsent(node.freq, f -> new DoublyLinkedList<>())
               .addFirst(node);
    }
    // Recompute minFreq
    minFreq = keyMap.values().stream()
        .mapToInt(n -> n.freq).min().orElse(0);
}
```

### How do you add TTL to the cache?

Store an expiry timestamp on each node. On access, check whether the entry has expired. If expired, treat it as a cache miss and remove the node. An optional background sweeper removes expired entries proactively.

```java
static class Node<K, V> {
    K key;
    V value;
    int freq;
    long expiresAt; // System.nanoTime() + TTL
    Node<K, V> prev, next;

    Node(K key, V value, long ttlNanos) {
        this.key = key;
        this.value = value;
        this.freq = 1;
        this.expiresAt = System.nanoTime() + ttlNanos;
    }

    boolean isExpired() {
        return System.nanoTime() > expiresAt;
    }
}
```

### How does this compare to LRU?

| Feature | LRU | LFU |
|---------|-----|-----|
| Eviction basis | Least recently used | Least frequently used |
| Strength | Adapts to shifting access patterns | Keeps genuinely popular entries |
| Weakness | One-time scan evicts hot entries | Old hot entries never leave (without decay) |
| Complexity | O(1) with HashMap + one DLL | O(1) with HashMap + frequency-bucketed DLLs |
| Production cache | LinkedHashMap, Guava | Caffeine (Window-TinyLFU hybrid) |

### What about thread safety in a banking service?

The promote and evict operations are multi-step and not atomic. In a concurrent service, guard the structure with a lock or use a proven concurrent cache library.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.Caffeine;
import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.Cache;
import java.time.Duration;

public class ProductionCache {
    // Caffeine: Window-TinyLFU with frequency sketch + periodic aging
    private static final Cache<String, BigDecimal> BALANCE_CACHE =
        Caffeine.newBuilder()
            .maximumSize(10_000)
            .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
            .build();

    public static BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        return BALANCE_CACHE.get(accountId,
            id -> fetchFromDatabase(id));
    }

    private static BigDecimal fetchFromDatabase(String accountId) {
        return new BigDecimal("50000.00"); // Simulated DB call
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Pure LFU suffers from cache pollution, which is why real caches like Caffeine use Window-TinyLFU, a hybrid of recency and frequency with a frequency sketch and periodic aging, giving better hit rates and bounded memory. In a concurrent banking service, always use a proven library rather than hand-rolling a cache with external locking.
